From 8e680afbd2a244cc89369c7ac1e1f8937aa5aacd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Maxime Jourdan <mjourdan@baylibre.com>
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 22:00:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] media: meson: vdec: add HEVC decode codec

Add the HEVC codec for the "HEVC" decoder core shared with VP9. Mainline
staging meson-vdec carries codec_hevc_common for that core but never
merged the HEVC codec on top of it.

This is Maxime Jourdan's codec from the "media: meson: vdec: add HEVC
decode codec" series, as carried and updated by LibreELEC for its
Amlogic kernels up to Linux 7.2 (LibreELEC.tv cc0aeeb6d6, before that
tree replaced the staging driver):

  amlogic-0052 FROMLIST(v2) add HEVC decode codec (Maxime Jourdan,
               Benjamin Roszak)
  amlogic-0060 fix memory leak of 'new_frame' (Christian Hewitt)
  amlogic-0066 multiple fixes for HEVC codec (Marek Czerski): signed
               POCs, wait for a random access point before decoding,
               resync after repeated missing references, and survive
               HEVC_DECPIC_DATA_ERROR instead of aborting

The LibreELEC version sits on top of amlogic-0051, which reworks the
compressed buffer helpers for 8-bit AFBC and changes amvdec_src_change()
to always queue a source change event. Neither is needed here: the codec
only uses compressed buffers for 10-bit streams, which the existing
helpers handle by downsampling into NV12, and the event change would
alter the H.264 path. The codec is adapted to the existing API instead,
and the SAO writer takes its chroma address and luma length from
codec_hevc_chroma_addr() and codec_hevc_luma_size(), so single-planar
NV12 works the same way it does for VP9.

On top of that, decoding on a Car Thing (G12A) turned up the following,
fixed here:

 - The MMU map, which 10-bit streams decode through on G12A and later,
   was only programmed at start, before the bit depth is known, and
   never once the map was allocated on resume. The decoder walked a
   stale page table and wrote compressed frames over unrelated memory,
   corrupting the slab allocator within milliseconds. Program it on
   resume.

 - Skipped slices, before a sync point or without a current picture,
   were dropped without telling the firmware, which waits to be told,
   so a stream that does not start on a random access point stalled
   for good. Skip them the way Amlogic's driver does: clear the wait
   flag, report HEVC_ACTION_DONE and kick the firmware. A picture
   skipped from its first slice never reaches CAPTURE, so also forget
   its packet with the new amvdec_drop_ts_offset(); otherwise the
   ESPARSER throttle counts it forever and stops feeding the decoder
   before it reaches a sync point.

 - Sync point handling and the deferred resync ran on every slice. On
   the second slice of a multi-slice IDR picture they output the half
   decoded picture, freed it, and dropped the rest of its slices. Only
   act on the first slice of a picture.

 - Pictures were completed with allow_drop false, so a packet that
   never produced a picture (skipped before a sync point, for instance)
   kept its timestamp and its ESPARSER count forever. The drain and resync paths
   completed pictures with the oldest timestamp rather than their own,
   which is wrong as soon as pictures are reordered. Complete every
   picture by bitstream offset and let unmatched packets age out.

 - Pictures decoded from missing references, or reported damaged with
   HEVC_DECPIC_DATA_ERROR, were returned as valid. Flag them with
   V4L2_BUF_FLAG_ERROR. RASL pictures of a CRA that decoding starts on
   reference pictures that were never decoded; skip them as the spec
   requires, instead of counting their missing references towards a
   resync that threw away the rest of the GOP.

 - Neither Amlogic's driver nor the firmware documents what follows
   HEVC_DECPIC_DATA_ERROR, and the firmware can also stop on a damaged
   stream without reporting anything. Abort the session when input
   beyond the tail the stream fetcher holds back has waited at the
   decoder for a second since the last interrupt or ESPARSER write. A
   paused or throttled stream lets the firmware drain the VIFIFO to
   that tail, so it is never mistaken for a stall. HEVC_OVER_DECODE,
   which aborted the session, is handled the way Amlogic's driver
   handles it: flag the picture and let the firmware move on.

 - Every picture was a 100 KiB order-5 kzalloc() from the IRQ thread,
   since struct hevc_frame held reference lists for 800 slices, and
   failed once memory was fragmented. Only the current slice's lists
   are ever read; keep just those. This also stops a picture with more
   than 800 slices from writing past the end of the array.

 - The workspace reserved 4K-sized motion vector buffers for 24
   pictures, about 27 MiB of CMA for any stream. Allocate them per
   CAPTURE buffer on resume, sized for the stream, about 5 MiB at 1080p.

 - Every stream asked for 16 CAPTURE buffers. Size the request from
   the SPS's sps_max_dec_pic_buffering, which the firmware reports
   after the fields above, the same way H.264 does. A typical 1080p
   stream needs 9 instead of 16, about 21 MiB less CMA. Firmware that
   does not report it keeps the old 16.

 - resume ran without the codec lock when called from start_streaming;
   take it there, and release it in the ISR before reporting a source
   change, which may resume right away. Free the workspace when the AUX
   buffer cannot be allocated.

 - The stream fetcher does not hand the last picture to the decoder
   until more input follows it, so on V4L2_DEC_CMD_STOP the last picture
   in decode order was flushed undecoded. Queue the same zero-padding
   end-of-stream sequence as VP9.

 - A picture still referenced after it is shown goes back to userspace
   and can be queued again, and the codec decoded the next picture
   into whichever CAPTURE buffer came first, overwriting the reference.
   Streams whose references outlive a few pictures (6 references, 8
   B-frames) drifted from the eleventh frame on. Skip ready buffers
   that hold a picture, as VP9 does.

Throttle the ESPARSER on available CAPTURE buffers for HEVC as for VP9,
but count the ready buffers that hold no reference instead of assuming
three: once the CAPTURE queue runs, feed a packet only when it has a
buffer to decode into.

Upstream-Status: Pending
Signed-off-by: Maxime Jourdan <mjourdan@baylibre.com>
Signed-off-by: Benjamin Roszak <benjamin545@gmail.com>
Signed-off-by: Christian Hewitt <christianshewitt@gmail.com>
Signed-off-by: Marek Czerski <m.czerski@ap-tech.pl>
Signed-off-by: Neel Patel <108803410+68p@users.noreply.github.com>
